  "summary": "A three-dimensional decorative element used in Islamic architecture, consisting of small niches stacked in tiers to create intricate honeycomb-like structures.",
  "description": "In Central Asian architecture, muqarnas were typically executed in g plaster or tile, decorating transitions between walls and domes, portals, and cornices. This technique allowed for complex spatial transitions while serving as a display of mathematical precision and artistic mastery, reflecting the region's sophisticated architectural heritage.",
  "culturalContext": "The muqarnas — honeycomb vaulting of stacked niche cells — reached sculptural perfection in Central Asian masterworks: Samarkand's Gur-e Amir and Bibi-Khanym portals, Bukhara's Balyand Mosque, the Shah-i-Zinda necropolis. Reading from below as dripping stalactites or frozen crystal, muqarnas dissolve the corner between square room and dome (squinch evolution), while gilded terracotta versions (Bukhara) made entire portal half-domes into suspended gardens of light. The geometry is modular craft: unit cells (kitab) assembled by master builders from pattern books.",
